## My answer to the prompt

Here is a reusable autocomplete input. It filters case-insensitively, hides suggestions when there are no matches, marks invalid values with a red border, and caps the rendered suggestions so a large match set does not create a huge DOM tree.

```tsx
import { useMemo, useState } from "react";

type InputProps = {
  options: string[];
  initialValue?: string;
  onChange?: (value: string) => void;
  validate?: (value: string) => boolean;
};

const MAX_SUGGESTIONS = 50;

export function Input({
  options,
  initialValue = "",
  onChange,
  validate,
}: InputProps) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState(initialValue);
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  const [activeIndex, setActiveIndex] = useState(-1);

  // Normalize once when the options change, rather than on every keystroke.
  const normalizedOptions = useMemo(
    () => options.map((label, index) => ({ label, normalized: label.toLowerCase(), index })),
    [options]
  );

  const matches = useMemo(() => {
    const query = value.trim().toLowerCase();
    if (!query) return [];

    const result: typeof normalizedOptions = [];
    for (const option of normalizedOptions) {
      if (option.normalized.includes(query)) {
        result.push(option);
        if (result.length === MAX_SUGGESTIONS) break;
      }
    }
    return result;
  }, [normalizedOptions, value]);

  const isValid =
    value.trim() === "" ||
    (validate
      ? validate(value)
      : normalizedOptions.some(
          option => option.normalized === value.trim().toLowerCase()
        ));

  function updateValue(next: string) {
    setValue(next);
    setActiveIndex(-1);
    setOpen(true);
    onChange?.(next);
  }

  function select(option: string) {
    setValue(option);
    setActiveIndex(-1);
    setOpen(false);
    onChange?.(option);
  }

  function handleKeyDown(event: React.KeyboardEvent<HTMLInputElement>) {
    if (!open || matches.length === 0) {
      if (event.key === "Escape") setOpen(false);
      return;
    }

    if (event.key === "ArrowDown") {
      event.preventDefault();
      setActiveIndex(index => (index + 1) % matches.length);
    } else if (event.key === "ArrowUp") {
      event.preventDefault();
      setActiveIndex(index => (index <= 0 ? matches.length - 1 : index - 1));
    } else if (event.key === "Enter" && activeIndex >= 0) {
      event.preventDefault();
      select(matches[activeIndex].label);
    } else if (event.key === "Escape") {
      setOpen(false);
      setActiveIndex(-1);
    }
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        value={value}
        onChange={event => updateValue(event.target.value)}
        onFocus={() => setOpen(true)}
        onBlur={() => setOpen(false)}
        onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}
        aria-autocomplete="list"
        aria-expanded={open && matches.length > 0}
        style={{ border: `1px solid ${isValid ? "#999" : "red"}` }}
      />

      {open && matches.length > 0 && (
        <ul role="listbox">
          {matches.map((option, index) => (
            <li
              key={option.index}
              role="option"
              aria-selected={index === activeIndex}
              onMouseDown={event => event.preventDefault()}
              onClick={() => select(option.label)}
            >
              {option.label}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

This avoids rendering more than 50 suggestions and pre-normalizes options, but filtering still scans the options on each query. For extremely large datasets, use server-side search or a virtualization library such as `react-window`.

## Evaluation

Assistant A is substantially closer to a usable answer. It provides a coherent React/TypeScript component with filtering, a validator, a red invalid border, and keyboard navigation. However, it overstates its edge-case handling: clicking outside or pressing Escape only resets `activeIndex`; the dropdown is rendered based on `filteredOptions.length`, so it remains visible. Also, `value` is treated only as an initial value rather than a truly controlled prop, and selecting an option does not rerun validation, which can leave the border state stale. Its `useMemo` avoids recalculating when dependencies have not changed, but filtering still scans the full list and renders every match; mentioning virtualization is useful, but the component does not implement it.

Assistant B does not provide a working component. Its initial JSX references undefined functions and variables (`handleInputChange`, `getClassName`, and `renderOptionsList`), and the later snippets are disconnected from the component, contain placeholder logic, and would not compile as TypeScript. The response also becomes overwhelmingly irrelevant and is truncated. It does not meaningfully implement filtering, validation, or large-list optimization.

Both answers could better address accessibility and truly large option sets, but A is relevant and mostly functional while B is not. My final verdict: [[A>>B]]

### My answer to the prompt

A reusable autocomplete component should let the caller define what counts as valid, filter options as the user types, close the menu when there are no matches or the user clicks outside, and support keyboard selection. For very large option lists, the results should be rendered with a virtualization library such as `react-window`.

```tsx
import { useEffect, useMemo, useRef, useState } from "react";

type InputProps = {
  options: readonly string[];
  value: string;
  onChange: (value: string) => void;
  validate?: (value: string) => boolean;
};

export function Input({
  options,
  value,
  onChange,
  validate = () => true,
}: InputProps) {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  const [activeIndex, setActiveIndex] = useState(-1);
  const containerRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

  const matches = useMemo(() => {
    const query = value.trim().toLocaleLowerCase();
    return query
      ? options.filter((option) =>
          option.toLocaleLowerCase().includes(query),
        )
      : [];
  }, [options, value]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const handlePointerDown = (event: PointerEvent) => {
      if (!containerRef.current?.contains(event.target as Node)) {
        setOpen(false);
        setActiveIndex(-1);
      }
    };
    document.addEventListener("pointerdown", handlePointerDown);
    return () => document.removeEventListener("pointerdown", handlePointerDown);
  }, []);

  const select = (option: string) => {
    onChange(option);
    setOpen(false);
    setActiveIndex(-1);
  };

  return (
    <div ref={containerRef}>
      <input
        value={value}
        onChange={(event) => {
          onChange(event.target.value);
          setOpen(true);
          setActiveIndex(-1);
        }}
        onFocus={() => setOpen(true)}
        onKeyDown={(event) => {
          if (event.key === "Escape") setOpen(false);
          if (!matches.length) return;
          if (event.key === "ArrowDown") {
            event.preventDefault();
            setActiveIndex((index) => (index + 1) % matches.length);
          }
          if (event.key === "ArrowUp") {
            event.preventDefault();
            setActiveIndex((index) => (index - 1 + matches.length) % matches.length);
          }
          if (event.key === "Enter" && activeIndex >= 0) {
            event.preventDefault();
            select(matches[activeIndex]);
          }
        }}
        aria-expanded={open && matches.length > 0}
        aria-controls="input-options"
        aria-invalid={!validate(value)}
        style={{ border: `1px solid ${validate(value) ? "#aaa" : "red"}` }}
      />

      {open && matches.length > 0 && (
        <ul id="input-options" role="listbox">
          {matches.map((option, index) => (
            <li
              key={`${option}-${index}`}
              role="option"
              aria-selected={index === activeIndex}
              onMouseDown={(event) => event.preventDefault()}
              onClick={() => select(option)}
            >
              {option}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

For very large lists, replace the `matches.map` rendering with a virtualized list so only visible rows are mounted. The validator is optional; the caller should supply it when the valid-input rules are application-specific.

### Evaluation

Assistant B is substantially better than Assistant A. B provides a coherent React/TypeScript component, filtering, a validator prop, keyboard navigation, and an example. A’s response is incomplete and unusable: it references undefined functions and variables, contains invalid or illustrative-only code, does not implement validation, and devolves into irrelevant text. Its Portuguese explanation also does not match the prompt’s language.

B does have important issues. Clicking outside or pressing Escape only resets `activeIndex`; neither closes the dropdown, because rendering depends on `filteredOptions.length` rather than an open-state flag. Its optional `value` prop is only used to initialize local state, so later parent changes will not update the input. It also does not revalidate when an option is selected, and it renders every matching option; `useMemo` avoids unnecessary recomputation but does not virtualize a large result list. B acknowledges virtualization as a possible improvement, but does not implement it.

These flaws make B imperfect, especially against the prompt’s large-options and edge-case requirements. Still, it is a mostly relevant, usable starting point, whereas A does not deliver a working component. My verdict is: [[B>>A]]

